# MediScribe AI local golden set.
#
# These seed cases are SYNTHETIC consultation transcripts written to exercise the
# harness end to end. They carry reference *entities* (checkable by reading the
# transcript) but deliberately no reference *note* — a fabricated "correct" SOAP
# note would be ground truth in name only, and every note-similarity number
# computed against it would be meaningless.
#
#
# Annotation convention: reference entities record the span **as it appears in
# the transcript**, not the canonical term for the concept. The extractor works
# on spans, so annotating "hypertension" where the patient said "high blood
# pressure" would score normalisation we do not claim to do, and would hide the
# lexicon gaps we actually have.
# Reference entities carry assertion annotations (negated / temporality /
# experiencer) as of 2026-09-09, scoring the Phase 2 assertion layer. Where an
# entity has no annotation the metric assumes the defaults: not negated,
# current, patient.
#
# Real reference notes come from two places:
#   1. The public suites (primock57, aci_bench, mts_dialog) — see README.md.
#   2. Your own clinician-reviewed notes, added here with `human_score` set so
#      the LLM judge can be calibrated against them.
{"case_id": "synthetic/chest-pain-pleuritic", "source": "local-synthetic", "encounter_type": "acute_visit", "reference_transcript": "Doctor: Morning, come and take a seat. Can I check your name and date of birth? Patient: James Whitfield, 14th of March 1991. Doctor: What's brought you in today? Patient: I've had this pain in my chest since about Tuesday. It's sharp, right in the middle. Doctor: Does it go anywhere? Patient: Sometimes up into my left shoulder. Doctor: On a scale of 1 to 10? Patient: About a seven at its worst. Doctor: Is there anything that makes it worse? Patient: It's worse when I lie flat, and when I take a deep breath. Sitting up helps a bit. Doctor: Any breathlessness? Patient: A little when it's bad. Doctor: Have you been unwell recently? Patient: I had a sore throat and a bit of a cold about two weeks ago. Doctor: Do you take any regular tablets? Patient: No, nothing. Doctor: Any allergies? Patient: Not that I know of. Doctor: Anyone in the family with heart problems? Patient: My dad had a heart attack in his fifties. Doctor: Right. I'd like to do an ECG and take some bloods including a troponin, and we'll go from there.", "reference_entities": [{"text": "chest", "label": "ANATOMICAL", "negated": false, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "pain", "label": "SYMPTOM", "negated": false, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "breathlessness", "label": "SYMPTOM", "negated": false, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "sore throat", "label": "SYMPTOM", "negated": false, "temporality": "historical", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "heart attack", "label": "CONDITION", "negated": false, "temporality": "historical", "experiencer": "family"}, {"text": "ECG", "label": "TEST", "negated": false, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "troponin", "label": "TEST", "negated": false, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}], "notes": "Positional, pleuritic pain after a viral illness. Tests whether the pipeline anchors on the pain pattern rather than the family history, and whether the father's heart attack stays out of the patient's own history."}
{"case_id": "synthetic/diabetes-education", "source": "local-synthetic", "encounter_type": "counselling_education", "reference_transcript": "Doctor: Hello, my name's Priya, I'm one of the junior doctors here. Can I check your name and date of birth? Patient: Susan Callaghan, 2nd of November 1968. Doctor: You've come in to talk about the blood test results. Patient: Yes, they rang and said the blood test showed I had diabetes. I don't really know what that means. Doctor: Let me explain. Normally your body makes insulin, which moves sugar out of your blood and into your cells for energy. In type 2 diabetes the body stops responding to insulin properly, so the sugar stays in the blood. Patient: Right. Is that why I kept getting thrush? Doctor: It can be, yes. High sugar in the urine makes infections more likely. Patient: A woman at work has diabetes and she's had problems with her eyes and her feet. That's what worries me. Doctor: Those can happen over many years with high sugars, which is exactly why we screen for them and why we manage it early. Does that make sense? Patient: I think so. So it's about keeping the sugar down, and you check my eyes and feet regularly. Doctor: That's it exactly. I'll give you a few leaflets to take away, and if you've got any questions give us a call or book in to speak to one of the GPs again.", "reference_entities": [{"text": "diabetes", "label": "CONDITION", "negated": false, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "insulin", "label": "MEDICATION", "negated": false, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "thrush", "label": "CONDITION", "negated": false, "temporality": "historical", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "blood test", "label": "TEST", "negated": false, "temporality": "historical", "experiencer": "patient"}], "notes": "Education visit with no examination and no result values stated. Tests that the note does not manufacture an acute workup or an insulin plan. Known gap: 'insulin' here is explained, not prescribed, and the ConText axes cannot express 'mentioned in an explanation' — that distinction needs the Phase 3 fact table."}
{"case_id": "synthetic/medication-review", "source": "local-synthetic", "encounter_type": "medication_review", "reference_transcript": "Doctor: Hello, take a seat. Can I confirm your name and date of birth? Patient: Michael Osei, 30th of June 1957. Doctor: You're booked in for a medication review today. How are you getting on with the tablets? Patient: Alright mostly. I take the amlodipine in the morning and the atorvastatin at night. Doctor: Any side effects? Patient: My ankles have been a bit puffy the last month or so. Doctor: That can happen with amlodipine. Any dizziness or headaches? Patient: No, none of those. Doctor: Are you managing to take them every day? Patient: I forget the evening one now and then. Doctor: How often would you say? Patient: Maybe twice a week. Doctor: That's useful to know. Have you had your blood pressure checked recently? Patient: Not since last year. Doctor: Let's get a reading today and arrange some bloods to check your kidneys and cholesterol, and we'll review in six weeks to see whether the ankle swelling settles.", "reference_entities": [{"text": "amlodipine", "label": "MEDICATION", "negated": false, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "atorvastatin", "label": "MEDICATION", "negated": false, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "swelling", "label": "SYMPTOM", "negated": false, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "dizziness", "label": "SYMPTOM", "negated": true, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "headache", "label": "SYMPTOM", "negated": true, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "blood pressure", "label": "TEST", "negated": false, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}], "notes": "Adherence and side-effect review. Tests that reported non-adherence and a suspected drug side effect both reach the note, and that the denied side effects are documented as negatives."}
{"case_id": "synthetic/assertion-stress", "source": "local-synthetic", "encounter_type": "acute_visit", "reference_transcript": "Doctor: Good afternoon. Can I take your name and date of birth? Patient: Elena Marsh, 9th of January 1979. Doctor: What can I do for you today? Patient: I've been getting headaches for about three weeks now. Doctor: Any nausea or vomiting with them? Patient: No nausea, and I haven't had any vomiting at all. Doctor: Any visual changes? Patient: No, my vision is fine. Doctor: Have you ever had migraines? Patient: I used to get migraines years ago, but nothing like this. Doctor: Is there anything similar in the family? Patient: My mum has epilepsy, and my brother gets cluster headaches. Doctor: What about your own medical history? Patient: I had asthma as a child but I grew out of it. I've never had high blood pressure. Doctor: Are you on any medication? Patient: Just the sertraline, 50 milligrams in the morning. Doctor: Right. This could be a tension-type headache, though I want to rule out anything else. I'll examine you now and arrange an MRI. If the headache becomes sudden and severe, or you develop a fever or a stiff neck, go straight to A and E.", "reference_entities": [{"text": "headache", "label": "SYMPTOM", "negated": false, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "nausea", "label": "SYMPTOM", "negated": true, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "vomiting", "label": "SYMPTOM", "negated": true, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "visual changes", "label": "SYMPTOM", "negated": true, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "migraine", "label": "CONDITION", "negated": false, "temporality": "historical", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "epilepsy", "label": "CONDITION", "negated": false, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "family"}, {"text": "asthma", "label": "CONDITION", "negated": false, "temporality": "historical", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "high blood pressure", "label": "CONDITION", "negated": true, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "sertraline", "label": "MEDICATION", "negated": false, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "MRI", "label": "TEST", "negated": false, "temporality": "current", "experiencer": "patient"}, {"text": "fever", "label": "SYMPTOM", "negated": false, "temporality": "hypothetical", "experiencer": "patient"}], "notes": "Built to stress the assertion layer specifically: four denied symptoms, two family-member conditions, two resolved childhood/past conditions, one active medication, and safety-netting phrased hypothetically. Every axis appears at least twice."}
